# gen_fu_irq_red_observability: the irq entry's designed fault, seen through the entry's own check

WHAT THIS CLAIMS. The fault gen_irq_basic_prog.py injects in its red form is OBSERVABLE through
gen_test_irq_basic's own line_facts, at every seed and for every red item, and it is attributable
to one item because no other line class fails. NOT claimed: any simulation. This is the
model-level half of the generator hand-off rule; the simulated 40-seed green sweep is the other
half and belongs to the Runtime Manager.

HOW IT AVOIDS BEING A MODEL OF ITSELF, which is what this kind of check invites:
  * The predicate is the ENTRY'S OWN line_facts, imported rather than copied, so a check that
    drifted from the test would not pass this.
  * The fault is PARSED out of the emitted program text, not read from the generator's own
    RED_VECTOR table, and a deviation block the reader does not recognise raises instead of being
    guessed at. Reading the table would have made the check agree with itself.
  * Every seed and every red item: a fault observable at most seeds is a fixture that passes at
    the rest, which the flow reports as a red fixture passing unexpectedly.
  * The green stream is the control on every armed line, so a checker that failed everything
    would not pass either.

